      "writtenOn": "2026-09-23T10:31:48Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yes, there is a use for every one of them.\n\n`emit_object_added` - this is the preferred default when you have your object fully constructed via the constructor.\n`emit_interface_added` - this is used when you have a sub-interface you have added dynamically at a path and is fully constructed.\n`defer_emit` - this is used when you need to set up member variables before you emit, which was used a lot in the sync bindings, but hopefully less now that we have the `properties_t` constructor form.\n`emit_no_signals` - this is used when you are setting up an `optional\u003cfoo\u003e` sub-interface, but it is all determined in the parent interface\u0027s constructor.  This is used as protection to ensure that the child/sub-interface never emits its own signals because it was handled by the parent\u0027s `emit_object_added`.\n\nA use case of the example I gave for `emit_no_signals` is the `extensions` behavior we are currently adding to phosphor-logging events in order to handle CPER data.",
